K2 by the letter: SURVEY − NOSURVEY = +0 → a basin, not a rate; the survey default STAYS ON. The J3 miss was one pinned basin (rb-10849's warning realized: pinned N=3 is one sample of one basin).
K4: the reading-order mechanism reproduces in every sampled basin (config first 6/6 without the survey; module or package first 6/6 with it) and the opening listing calls go 3 → 0 in every run, without moving the pass rate; turns medians 13.0 vs 13.5, wall 196 vs 217 s.
Failure class in the sampled basins is NOT arm J's docstring rule (0 of 12 runs failed the lint; all 10 passes "pass the lint pyproject.toml declares"): both failures are an update_plan DOOM LOOP — after write_file(duration.py) the model issued 7–9 consecutive update_plan calls with no other tool, the stuck ladder fired (stuck 1–3, doom_recovery 1) and the run ended stop_reason=doom_loop before utils/__init__.py exported the function and before any test was written (verifier: "parse_duration is not exported from the utils package"). A third doom_loop (SURVEY b2 run3) ended AFTER the work was done and passed. Interventions per row recorded through #427/#428: plan, plan_review, suite_scope_gate, plan_first, stuck, doom_recovery, doom_loop — the last three had never been recorded by the bench before this arm.
Consequence: arm L (verify_auto) would exercise its gate only in basins that produce lint-rejected code, which 0 of 12 sampled basins did; its pre-registration stays drafted and unlaunched until a basin census says the gate can fire. The live lever on this task is the update_plan doom loop — measured next from the dumps before any code change.
